one of the greatest opportunities we  have particularly from the perspective  of the university is how can we improve  human health globally today 30,000  children under the age of 5 die every  day  it's an enormous number and it's  avoidable but when we need to avoid that  we really have to learn how to create  the evidence and disseminate the  evidence from a university to the field  where these stuffs are happening where  the health is really not as strong as it  could be one of the ways in which Yale  is doing this is we have something  called the Global Health Leadership  Institute and its major mission is to  develop strong relationships between  Yale faculty who are producing new  evidence and the actual field the  delegations that come from the country  policymakers practitioners communities  from the country where we can really  make a link between the Yale faculty who  are producing what needs these countries  need to take up and the country  leadership themselves an example that's  a good one to think about is the work  that Yale is doing in Ghana through the  global health Leadership Institute both  with the economic growth center and the  School of Public Health faculty here  engaged with faculty at the University  of Ghana have been able to document that  nearly seven percent of the GDP in Ghana  has been foregone in large part due to  the mental health problems and the  psychological distress which translates  into people not being able to work  questions asked to community members in  Ghana how many days have you been unable  to work due to your emotional distress  in the last month have helped us  document exactly how much of the GDP has  been for gone this is an incredibly  important problem for the first time the  President of Ghana was able to say I  could invest in mental health not just  because it's the right thing to do but  it makes economic sense  it will help development in Ghana and  that's something we can do in multiple  countries with getting faculty engaged  with the actual policymakers in these  countries
 

